{:edb/db-before-t 1530 :edb/db-after-t 1531 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534843 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:48:28.365Z" 13194139534843 true] [17592186166634 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\, \\rm{ m/s}$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\sin^2 x \\,\\rm{m/s}^2.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ what differential equation describes this motion?" 13194139534843 true] [17592186166634 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\, \\rm{m/s}$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\sin^2 x \\,\\rm{m/s}^2$. Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O$, what differential equation describes this motion?" 13194139534843 false] [17592186166634 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/f187eff194f136838b67ab12b44f8f6882eafb59885a5c3f440d41492def4a42.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186166634 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3235/Source/Images/cd73ecc46cd29774b30dd48cc477c4d3.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{dv}{dx} \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt} =-\\sin^{2} x\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x\n$$" 13194139534843 false] [17592186166635 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$, away from a fixed origin $O$. When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x \\,\\rm{m}$, its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2$. If $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m$, find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 1 \\, \\textrm m$. Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139534843 false] [17592186166635 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x \\,\\rm{m},$ its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2.$ If $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 1 \\, \\textrm m.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139534843 true] [17592186166635 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/4fdd70a4ca80965c0adce9c60be36b0712852b662cff69f9733f50c739fd1b32.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{e}^{x-2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = x^2 + e^{x-2} + C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{e}^{2-2}+C \\\\ 8 & =4+1+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x) \\gt 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{2\\left(x^2 + e^{x-2} + 3\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x = 1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({1}^{2}+{e}^{1-2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(4+{e}^{-1})} \\\\  & ≈2.96\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139534843 true] [17592186166635 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3235/Source/Images/adcc30a786d5323ec9b2c92769b6d11e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\,\\rm{m/s}^2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2x + e^{x-2}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x + e^{x-2}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2})dx &= ∫(2x + e^{x - 2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = x^2 + e^{x-2} + C\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx = 2 \\, \\textrm m\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} &= 2^{2} + e^{2 - 2} + C \\\\\n8 &= 4 + 1 + C \\\\\nC &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= x^{2} + e^{x - 2} + 3 \\\\\nv^{2} &= 2(x^{2} + e^{x - 2} + 3) \\\\\nv &= ± \\sqrt{2(x^{2} + e^{x - 2} + 3)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we have that $v(x) \\gt 0$, so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{2\\left(x^2 + e^{x-2} + 3\\right)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x = 1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv &= \\sqrt{2(1^{2} + e^{1 - 2} + 3)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2(4 + e^{-1})} \\\\\n&\\approx 2.96m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139534843 false] [17592186166644 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\geq 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\textrm m$, the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed toward $O$. Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$. Find $x$ as a function of time $t$." 13194139534843 false] [17592186166644 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\geq 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\textrm m,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186166644 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/eca36626e6a0cfb30d293e684483c37ddce6f18d4004c3f77258e9663565d8d7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =-\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=0\\, \\textrm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =\\frac{2}{0+1}+K \\\\ 2 & =2+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x) > 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}} \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1)^{3/2} & =2t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = \\dfrac{3C_1}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/2} =3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{2/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{2/3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186166644 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3235/Source/Images/10ca9059211ec926ef3e8403f61e367f.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2\n$$\n\nand is directed *toward* $O$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =-\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2})dx &= ∫ - (2)/((x + 1)^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= \\frac{2}{x + 1} + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nv=2\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nwhen\n\n$$\nx=0\\, \\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nwe can find the value of the constant $K$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= \\frac{2}{x + 1} + K \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} &= \\frac{2}{0 + 1} + K \\\\\n2 &= 2 + K \\\\\nK &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}v^{2} &= \\frac{2}{x + 1} \\\\\nv^{2} &= \\frac{4}{x + 1} \\\\\nv &= ± \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x + 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x$, we have that $v(x) > 0$, so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nv= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x + 1}} \\\\\n\\sqrt{x + 1}\\frac{dx}{dt} &= 2 \\\\\n∫\\sqrt{x + 1}\\frac{dx}{dt}dt &= ∫2dt \\\\\n∫\\sqrt{x + 1}dx &= ∫2dt \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}(x + 1)^{3/2} &= 2t + C_{1} \\\\\n(x + 1)^{3/2} &= 3t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{3C_1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0$. Therefore, $C=1$, and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/2} =3t+1\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 1)^{3/2} &= 3t + 1 \\\\\nx + 1 &= (3t + 1)^{2/3} \\\\\nx &= (3t + 1)^{2/3} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534843 false] [17592186166782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314053 13194139534843 true] [17592186166782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314060 13194139534843 true] [17592186166782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314074 13194139534843 true] [17592186166782 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314081 13194139534843 true] [17592186166783 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314095 13194139534843 true] [17592186166783 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314102 13194139534843 true] [17592186166783 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314123 13194139534843 true] [17592186166791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314067 13194139534843 true] [17592186166791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314088 13194139534843 true] [17592186166791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314109 13194139534843 true] [17592186166791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314116 13194139534843 true] [17592186166791 1020 #edb/ref 17592186314130 13194139534843 true] [17592186167000 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534843 true] [17592186167000 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $xm$, its velocity is $vm/s$ and its acceleration is $4xm/s^{2}$. If $P$ has a displacement of $2m$ when its velocity is $8m/s$, find $v$ in terms of $x$." 13194139534843 false] [17592186167000 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $2\\,m$ when its velocity is $8\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186167000 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=2\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=8\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =2(2)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =32-8 \\\\ C & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+24 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+48 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}+12) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\n$$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186167001 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534843 true] [17592186167001 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$, away from a fixed origin $O$. When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $xm$, its velocity is $vm/s$ and its acceleration is $(2 + 3e^{-x})m/s^{2}$. If $v = 2m/s$ when $x = 0m$, find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 3m$. Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139534843 false] [17592186167001 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=3\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." 13194139534843 true] [17592186167001 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/ec4af1357e75dcee6d7d5412467c2ee2ea9e54fa38fb6e83817b0ae909c529aa.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+3{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x-3{e}^{-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =2(0)-3{e}^{-0}+C \\\\ 2 & =-3+C \\\\ C & =2+3 \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x-3{e}^{-x}+5 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4x-6{e}^{-x}+10 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4(3)-6{e}^{-3}+10} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{22-6{e}^{-3}} \\\\ v & ≈4.7\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139534843 true] [17592186167003 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534843 true] [17592186167003 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x > 0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $vm/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $6x^{3}m/s^{2}$. Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O$, which differential equation describes this motion?" 13194139534843 false] [17592186167003 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $6{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" 13194139534843 true] [17592186167003 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/52fc197612dac35373ecebf771b8444505902b5c6e00c7c55d2b21dab4809136.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $6{x}^{3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314053 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314054 13194139534843 true] [17592186314053 1089 "q-122305" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314053 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534843 true] [17592186314053 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{-2x}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314053 1113 #uuid "985c21fe-e314-5497-89ec-0da4b5f6b75a" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314053 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/dd924518ae28ee8bf010e76b95ddbd68a26137b8b1f28753900455a87e1df799.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{-2x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314054 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314055 13194139534843 true] [17592186314054 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314056 13194139534843 true] [17592186314054 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314057 13194139534843 true] [17592186314054 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314058 13194139534843 true] [17592186314054 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314059 13194139534843 true] [17592186314054 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534843 true] [17592186314054 1041 "selection" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314054 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314056 13194139534843 true] [17592186314054 1110 #uuid "23013f89-b89e-5b9a-a50d-5890430c37c8" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314055 1034 "2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314055 1111 #uuid "d2a0c9ee-1435-58ad-9db8-1f41c5a1608a" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314056 1034 "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314056 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314056 1111 #uuid "6336ee57-0862-5ed8-87c5-d97d62829324" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314057 1034 "4{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314057 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314057 1111 #uuid "f5a83a2d-1a74-51a5-9b5e-2e254e317731" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314058 1034 "{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314058 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314058 1111 #uuid "88bee9e1-399b-58ee-8989-94bbd93457c5" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314059 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314059 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314059 1111 #uuid "05b1ee62-939c-5834-bf2f-140c3581deb3" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314060 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314061 13194139534843 true] [17592186314060 1089 "q-122310" 13194139534843 tr