Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314088 1113 #uuid "1f818b71-1c62-567d-b5ef-4300712765ba" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314088 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/8faba5a783797f7025f51ec1ae587cb68355dff7138278689346c2f713275562.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{-2x}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x} & =t+K \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =2t+C \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1).\n$$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314089 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314090 13194139534843 true] [17592186314089 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314091 13194139534843 true] [17592186314089 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314092 13194139534843 true] [17592186314089 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314093 13194139534843 true] [17592186314089 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314094 13194139534843 true] [17592186314089 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534843 true] [17592186314089 1041 "selection" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314089 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314091 13194139534843 true] [17592186314089 1110 #uuid "b1a84703-3c19-5a54-b80b-5eeb8b58eb55" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314090 1034 "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314090 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314090 1111 #uuid "5acf374f-291a-5639-a46b-7acac2eef5a9" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314091 1034 "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314091 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314091 1111 #uuid "b8f8ec42-40f1-55df-a2d2-6d0cfd24fbfd" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314092 1034 "x(t)=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314092 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314092 1111 #uuid "01be0c81-03f1-5b16-afe3-01d09fe19fe2" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314093 1034 "x(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314093 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314093 1111 #uuid "3c860876-8077-5d8a-b490-1124115ea5ce" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314094 1034 "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4t+1)" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314094 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314094 1111 #uuid "8fc2e683-94fa-5538-ac1d-59eb33666c12" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314095 1017 #edb/ref 17592186314096 13194139534843 true] [17592186314095 1089 "q-122337" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314095 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534843 true] [17592186314095 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $3\\,m$ when its velocity is $2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314095 1113 #uuid "6b97a4f6-ee90-5fc9-94b5-f52f95caa841" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314095 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/164c90893a07ae9657e90b6f2b25e53c9c4927b1f4a076429ba0521a98377f24.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={e}^{x-3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=3\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & ={e}^{3-3}+C \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+1 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({e}^{x-3}+1) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\n$$" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314097 13194139534843 true] [17592186314096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314098 13194139534843 true] [17592186314096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314099 13194139534843 true] [17592186314096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314100 13194139534843 true] [17592186314096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314101 13194139534843 true] [17592186314096 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534843 true] [17592186314096 1041 "selection" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314096 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314097 13194139534843 true] [17592186314096 1110 #uuid "ea53bf7f-7eb2-545c-a38e-16d9d8e43c05" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314097 1034 "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314097 1111 #uuid "56fe543e-e026-5266-a271-24932d9b1e27" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314098 1034 "v(x)=\\sqrt{3{e}^{x-3}+1}" 13194139534843 true] [1759