{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{e}^{x/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since  $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =2{e}^{0/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{x/2}+1 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4{e}^{1/2}+1} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{e}+1} \\\\  & ≈2.8\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139534843 true] [17592186314103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314104 13194139534843 true] [17592186314103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314105 13194139534843 true] [17592186314103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314106 13194139534843 true] [17592186314103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314107 13194139534843 true] [17592186314103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186314108 13194139534843 true] [17592186314103 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534843 true] [17592186314103 1041 "selection" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314103 1104 #edb/ref 17592186314106 13194139534843 true] [17592186314103 1110 #uuid "73602914-89c6-539e-8096-7272b5eeb3b0" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314104 1034 "3.3\\,\\text{m/s}" 13194139534843 true] [17592186314104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534843 true] [17592186314104 1111 #uuid "3221189b-a6e2-50f8-962a-f1b66bf2d35b" 13194139534843 true] [175