\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2(x+2)^{3}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2)^{4}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =(x+2)^{4} \\\\ v & =\\pm (x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x+2} & =t+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+2}=-t+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{1-2t}{2} \\\\ x+2 & =\\frac{2}{1-2t} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-2t}-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4t}{1-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13